<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo noupdate="1">

    <!--
        Landed cost services for imported merchandise.

        Without these, the cost of a carton of diapers is the supplier's invoice
        price only, and every sale is credited with a margin it did not earn -
        typically 15-30% overstated on sea-freighted consumer goods. Margin
        reporting built on that is fiction, and it is the single most common way
        a small importer discovers it has been selling at a loss.

        SPLIT METHOD IS A REAL DECISION, NOT A FORMALITY.

        Diapers, wet wipes and tissue are all LOW-DENSITY cargo: a container
        fills up - "cubes out" - long before it reaches its weight limit. Freight
        on cube-out cargo is charged against volume, whether that is an FCL rate
        for the box or a revenue-ton rate that resolves to CBM. So freight, port
        handling and clearing split BY VOLUME.

        Duty and insurance are assessed on declared value, so those split by
        cost. Bank and LC charges are a percentage of the invoice, so likewise.

        Getting this backwards quietly misprices every product in a mixed
        shipment while the total stays correct - which is exactly what makes it
        hard to notice.

        Valid split methods (stock_landed_costs/models/stock_landed_cost.py):
          equal | by_quantity | by_current_cost_price | by_weight | by_volume

        THE SPLIT METHOD BELOW IS A DEFAULT, NOT A LOCK. It is editable on each
        landed-cost line. Override it when a shipment is genuinely different:
        a container of dense goods that hits the weight limit should split
        freight by_weight, and a specific (per-kg or per-unit) customs duty
        should split by_weight or by_quantity rather than by value.

        TWO THINGS SILENTLY BREAK LANDED COSTS.

        1. Costing method. Odoo only adjusts value when costing is AVCO or FIFO
           (the guards on cost_method in ('fifo','average')). Under standard
           costing the adjustment is skipped outright. Every stocked category
           here is AVCO for exactly this reason - do not change one to standard.

        2. A zero on the field being split by. A product with volume 0 receives
           none of a by_volume allocation and everything else in the container
           absorbs its share; the same is true of weight under by_weight. The
           totals still balance, which is what hides it. Both `weight` and
           `volume` are therefore mandatory columns on every product CSV, so
           either split method works without silently zeroing a line.

        Each of these products still needs an expense account - the Services
        category carries none by design, so set one per product with the
        accountant when the chart of accounts is wired (README Phase 1 step 3).
        Customs Duty & Clearing (502100 in data/phase1-trading/accounts.csv) is
        the natural home for most of them.
    -->

    <record id="product_lc_sea_freight" model="product.template">
        <field name="name">Sea Freight</field>
        <field name="default_code">SRV-LC-001</field>
        <field name="type">service</field>
        <field name="categ_id" ref="categ_services"/>
        <field name="landed_cost_ok" eval="True"/>
        <field name="split_method_landed_cost">by_volume</field>
        <field name="purchase_ok" eval="True"/>
        <field name="sale_ok" eval="False"/>
    </record>

    <record id="product_lc_customs_duty" model="product.template">
        <field name="name">Customs Duty</field>
        <field name="default_code">SRV-LC-002</field>
